---
/**
 * FinalCta — homepage section 6, "See it on your own tender."
 *
 * DESIGN: THE LEGACY CTA, ported. Owner decision, 2026-08-02, recorded in
 * specs/HOMEPAGE-DECISION-TABLE.md: "Keep the legacy CTA from the live
 * homepage." Six Figma variants (C1-C6) were rendered and NONE was taken,
 * including the one I recommended. Nothing from C1-C6 may be carried across
 * here by halves. The source is `index.html` at the repo root, section 8,
 * `.nt-cta-banner`.
 *
 * WHAT WAS PORTED. The eyebrow "Ready when you are"; the heading "See it on your
 * own tender."; both actions and both hrefs; and the free-tool row with both of
 * its links. The card chrome: near-black two-stop wash, one hairline, pill
 * radius, and the three blurred screen-blended blobs that are what give the
 * production card its glow rather than leaving it a flat dark panel.
 *
 * THE BODY WAS SHORTENED, 2026-08-02, AND ITS TWO CLAIMS BOTH SURVIVED. The
 * legacy standfirst ran 25 words; the sitewide cut brought it to 16. What was
 * removed was "through the engine, end to end, on your own evidence", which is
 * scene-setting. What was NOT removed, and may not be, is either of the two
 * trust signals named below: "we will run it with you" and "Access is limited
 * and by request". A shorter sentence that dropped one of those would be a
 * different promise, not a tighter one.
 *
 * THE TRUST SIGNALS ARE THE COPY, and there are exactly two, both true.
 * "Access is limited and by request" and "Bring a live notice and we will run
 * it through the engine with you" are statements the company makes about its
 * own terms and its own service. They are unverifiable from outside, and that
 * is fine: a company may describe its own policy. What is NOT here, and must
 * never be added, is the other kind — customer names, logos, testimonials,
 * ratings, counts of users. There are none, so there are none on the page. Nor
 * is there a win-rate claim, here or anywhere: refusing to claim one is the
 * product's position, not an omission to be tidied up later.
 *
 * REQUEST ACCESS IS A WEB FORM AND NEVER A BARE mailto:. Standing rule. The
 * href carries `?enquiry=limited-access`, which is a WIRE VALUE read by
 * /contact to preselect the subject, and `#contact-form` to land on the form
 * rather than the top of that page. Neither is copy; do not "tidy" them.
 *
 * TWO DOORS, DELIBERATELY. A high-commitment one (request access, book a demo)
 * and a zero-commitment one (the free calculator and the methodology behind
 * it). The free row is the only no-account route off this page and it stays
 * even though it sends people out of the funnel, because a reader with no next
 * step does not convert later, they leave.
 *
 * THE HEAD BLOCK COMES FROM Section, NOT FROM HERE. Centred head blocks and the
 * gradient capsule eyebrow are owner decisions implemented once in
 * layout/Section.astro. The previous build of this file hand-rolled its own
 * eyebrow, heading and measure, which is how a design system dies quietly. The
 * card is a wrapper AROUND that primitive, so it decorates the head block
 * without redeclaring one property of it.
 *
 * TWO THINGS FROM THE LEGACY MARKUP ARE DELIBERATELY NOT PORTED:
 *   - `<span class="paid">` put a teal-to-violet gradient on the last three
 *     words of the heading. Section owns heading rendering and takes a plain
 *     string; reproducing the span would mean restating heading styles here,
 *     which is the exact duplication this component was rewritten to remove.
 *   - `.nt-reveal`, the entrance animation. Content that fades in is briefly
 *     unreadable by design and permanently unreadable when the observer does
 *     not fire, which has already shipped on this site twice.
 *
 * NO JAVASCRIPT. Every word, both buttons and both free links are in the HTML
 * and painted at first render.
 */
import Section from '../layout/Section.astro';
import Button from '../ui/Button.astro';
---

<div class="fc surface">
  {/*
    The mesh. Three blurred blobs INSIDE the card, screen-blended — teal high
    left, violet-deep low right, cyan across the top. This is the whole
    difference between the production card and a flat dark rectangle, and it was
    lost once already when this section was ported as semantic markup and the
    decorative layer was dropped with it.
  */}
  <div class="fc__mesh" aria-hidden="true">
    <b class="fc__m1"></b>
    <b class="fc__m2"></b>
    <b class="fc__m3"></b>
  </div>

  <div class="fc__in">
    <Section
      eyebrow="Ready when you are"
      title="See it on your own tender."
      standfirst="Bring a live notice and we will run it with you. Access is limited and by request."
      id="final-cta"
    >
      <div class="fc__actions">
        <Button href="/contact?enquiry=limited-access#contact-form">Request access</Button>
        <Button href="/contact" variant="secondary">Book a demo</Button>
      </div>

      {/*
        BOTH LINKS SURVIVE THE CUT, and that is deliberate rather than an
        oversight against the one-link-per-section target. They are the only
        no-account route off this page, and the methodology link is what makes
        the calculator checkable rather than a black box. Only the words got
        shorter.
      */}
      <p class="fc__free">
        <a href="/tools/ppn-002-calculator/">Score a bid, free</a>
        <a href="/tools/ppn-002-calculator/methodology/">How scoring works</a>
      </p>
    </Section>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  /*
   * THE CARD SITS ON THE MEASURE, LIKE THE LEGACY ONE. `width` plus a token
   * max-width rather than a bare max-width: at 390px a measure-width card with
   * `margin-inline: auto` runs to both viewport edges, and the closing section
   * of the homepage is the last place to ship an element bleeding off screen.
   * The gutter is subtracted here; Section then applies its own gutter inside,
   * so the copy is inset from the card edge by exactly one gutter at every
   * width. Nothing about the measure or the padding is redeclared.
   */
  .fc {
    position: relative;
    width: calc(100% - 2 * var(--gutter));
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-inline: auto;
    /* Clear of the section above, which ends on its own --sec-pad-y. */
    margin-block: 0 var(--sec-pad-y);
    border: 1px solid var(--c-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    /* A two-stop near-black wash leaning violet at the far corner. A flat fill
       is the strongest PowerPoint tell there is. */
    background: linear-gradient(
      135deg,
      var(--c-panel),
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-panel) 86%, var(--c-violet-deep))
    );
    /* Keeps the blobs inside the radius. They are sized to overhang. */
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .fc__mesh {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .fc__mesh b {
    position: absolute;
    border-radius: 50%;
    filter: blur(72px);
    /* Screen, not normal: these are lights on a dark panel, and normal blending
       would paint three grey discs over it. */
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
  }

  .fc__m1 {
    width: 420px;
    height: 420px;
    left: -6%;
    top: -44%;
    background: var(--orb-teal);
  }

  .fc__m2 {
    width: 400px;
    height: 400px;
    right: -6%;
    bottom: -48%;
    background: var(--orb-violet);
  }

  .fc__m3 {
    width: 300px;
    height: 300px;
    left: 44%;
    top: -32%;
    background: radial-gradient(
      circle,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-cyan) 32%, transparent),
      transparent 68%
    );
  }

  /* Above the mesh. Without this the blobs paint over the copy. */
  .fc__in {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
  }

  .fc__actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }

  /*
   * THE FREE ROW, in the legacy treatment: outlined pills rather than plain
   * underlined links, so the zero-commitment door reads as a pair of choices
   * next to the two buttons instead of as body copy that happens to be blue.
   *
   * Legacy set these at 12.5px. They are --t-body here. The type scale was
   * lifted deliberately after the owner read the homepage as small and tight,
   * and re-importing a fixed 12.5px would put one row back under the old scale
   * on the page's most important control cluster.
   */
  .fc__free {
    margin: var(--sec-gap) 0 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 10px;
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  .fc__free a {
    /* 44px, WCAG 2.2 target size. Legacy shipped these at 6px padding on a
       12.5px line, which is roughly 26px tall and fails 2.5.8. */
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: 0 14px;
    border: 1px solid var(--c-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color var(--dur) var(--ease), border-color var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }

  .fc__free a:hover {
    color: var(--c-teal);
    border-color: var(--c-border-glow);
  }

  .fc__free a:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--c-teal);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }

  /*
   * At 390px the blobs are wider than the card and the blur pushes the wash to
   * a near-flat violet, which loses the panel's edges rather than lighting it.
   * They are scaled with the card, not switched off: the glow is the design.
   */
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .fc__m1, .fc__m2 { width: 260px; height: 260px; }
    .fc__m3 { width: 200px; height: 200px; }
    .fc__mesh b { filter: blur(56px); }
  }
</style>
